set(v_is_dead,0)
 

"Let me see if I have this straight," Hadrik says, leaning across the table toward Meridith. "You people have been siphoning energy from mages across the continent since day one, all to kill Eiden."

"Yes," Meridith says.

"And if your little machine doesn't finish the job, you want us as your scavengers," Hadrik says. "Put him down like an injured dog once you've bled him dry. That's the offer?"

"That is... not how I would phrase it, but in essence, yes," Meridith says. Her hands stay flat on the stone.

"Do you have no honor?" Hadrik's mouth curls in raw disgust.

"Perhaps there has been a misunderstanding," Meridith says, shifting back half an inch. "If you permit me to explain the necessity once more, I am certain we can reach an agreement—"

"No, I've heard enough." Hadrik shoves his chair back. The legs screech against the floor.

Diane tenses, pushing up from her seat, but Meridith snaps an arm across her chest, holding her down.

"You and your underlings can scurry through the dirt with your traps all you like, but I want no part of it," Hadrik says. "Find another butcher for your dirty work."

He turns on his heel and storms out into the corridor, broad shoulders stiff, without glancing back.

"Hadrik! Hey—wait up!" Daren calls after him, getting only heavy boot-falls echoing off the seredium.

"I need to go after him," Daren says, turning to Meridith. "The last thing we need is him running into your rival factions while his blood is up. I'll bring him back if I can."

"There is no need," Meridith says, smoothing her robes as she gathers her composure. "We will resume later. You all have matters to consider."

"Fine," Daren says. "Later, then. Let me catch him before he tears a door off."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Intro2
TEXT:

"Wait for us!" Flower calls out, hopping out of her chair to trail Daren toward the doorway. "We're coming!"

"Actually, I would like a word in private with my nephew and Kate, if you do not mind," Meridith says. "It will only take a moment."

Illuna and Melindra stop at the threshold. Their eyes narrow in unison, locked on Meridith and Diane. Neither looks eager to leave us isolated in a room carved out of seredium.

"It's fine," Kate tells them quietly. "Go ahead. We'll catch up."

"If you say so," Melindra says with a brief nod. "Come on, Flower."

The two of them slip into the hallway. Meridith waits until the footsteps fade down the corridor before turning back to us.

"I have a question for you two," Meridith says. "Where does your group stand on Eiden? Do your companions view him as an ally or an enemy?"

"Isn't that something you should have asked before ordering us to execute him?" Kate asks, brow furrowed.

"You are correct," Meridith admits. "I should have asked sooner. I simply never considered that anyone could take that monster's side—aside from Melindra, given their history. But your dwarf companion's reaction forces me to reassess. Beyond his talk of clan honor, Hadrik seemed genuinely reluctant to see Eiden dead. Enlighten me. Why?"

choice(""Eiden has helped us on several occasions, so it's hard to consider him an enemy."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 0)
choice(""Eiden has acted friendly with us in the past, but I've never trusted him."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 1)
choice(""I'd say that Eiden is neither our friend nor our foe, at the moment."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Suspicious
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 0) {
"Eiden has helped us on several occasions, so it's hard to consider him an enemy."

"Is that why he trusted you with a message for me?" Meridith asks. "Because he views you as allies?"

"I doubt Eiden views anyone as an ally," Kate says. "The only reason we're alive is that we aren't a threat to him. That changes the second he learns we agreed to help you butcher him."

"There is no reason Eiden should ever learn of our agreement," Meridith says. "We are buried under tons of seredium rock. Even his senses cannot pierce these depths."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly from auras," Kate counters. "If he suspects even a fraction of the truth, three questions are all he needs to pull the whole plot out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith says, her shoulders dropping slightly. "Forgive me. In my haste to eliminate him, I nearly put you in mortal danger. Perhaps a different arrangement is in order—one even your dwarf might stomach."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 1) {
"Eiden has acted friendly with us in the past, but I've never trusted him."

"You distrust him, but what of the others?" Meridith asks. "Do they share your assessment?"

"None of us trusts Eiden," Kate says. "But provoking him without cause while he leaves us alone is suicidal. If he decides to fight us at full power, we lose in seconds."

"There is no need for open provocation," Meridith says. "Guard our secret, and you may treat with him exactly as you have before."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly from auras," Kate counters. "If he suspects even a fraction of the truth, three questions are all he needs to pull the whole plot out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith says, her shoulders dropping slightly. "Forgive me. In my haste to eliminate him, I nearly put you in mortal danger. Perhaps a different arrangement is in order—one even your dwarf might stomach."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 2) {
"I'd say that Eiden is neither our friend nor our foe, at the moment."

"I see," Meridith says, tapping her chin. "And does the rest of your party agree?"

"I agree with Barry," Kate says. "Eiden hasn't tried to kill us lately, but we haven't forgotten that he nearly ended us on a whim. That said, picking a fight with him is suicide. If he turns his full power on us, we're gone."

"There is no need for open provocation," Meridith says. "Guard our secret, and you may treat with him exactly as you have before."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly from auras," Kate counters. "If he suspects even a fraction of the truth, three questions are all he needs to pull the whole plot out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith says, her shoulders dropping slightly. "Forgive me. In my haste to eliminate him, I nearly put you in mortal danger. Perhaps a different arrangement is in order—one even your dwarf might stomach."

}
"What kind of deal?" I ask.

"Continue participating in the tournament," Meridith says. "That ensures you remain in Varathia when our strike lands. Keep clearing objectives so the kings cannot expel you, and I guarantee your access to our Magium node—assuming our strike kills Eiden without your help. If he survives, the choice is yours. Finish him, and the node is yours; refuse, and you walk away with nothing. But I will not ask for your answer until that moment arrives. If Eiden questions you, your auras will remain entirely clean."

"Are you sure you want to offer that?" Kate asks. "You're taking all the risk."

"Granting access to the node costs me very little," Meridith says. "Failing to kill Eiden costs me everything. If this bargain increases our odds by a fraction of a percent, it is worth every concession."

"The others will likely accept," Kate says. "Most of them stayed in the tournament for their own reasons anyway."

"I hope you are right," Meridith says.

"On another note," I say, stepping forward. "You mentioned earlier that you have the override codes to unlock the magical stats on my booster. Could you—"

"Of course," Meridith says, holding out a hand. "Pass it here. Though I should warn you: those two stats function very differently from the twelve you are accustomed to."

"Differently how?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Augmenting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Augmenting)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Augmenting
TEXT:

"Magical Power and Magical Knowledge were the first two stats built into the prototypes," Meridith explains. "The lessathi who designed them wanted to give our people the ability to cast magic. After decades of failure, they realized pure lessathi cannot channel spells like natural mages. Only half-lessathi could ever use them. Finding the stats useless to their own kind, our ancestors locked them behind a cipher and engineered twelve replacement stats with entirely different mechanisms."

"Different how?" I ask. "The device still extracts energy from mages and converts it into stat points, doesn't it?"

"Your premise is flawed," Meridith says. "Physical stats amplify what your body already possesses—strength, speed, hearing, reflexes—by replicating generalist enhancement spells. But information stats like ancient languages and combat technique do not modify you at all. They store vast libraries in the device's aura matrix. When you invest points, the booster permits a controlled stream of that data to interface with your mind without burning out your brain. Sever the connection, and the knowledge vanishes."

"Right," I say. "So the booster acts as an external catalyst. Physical stats mimic enhancement spells, aura stats copy standard manipulation techniques, and information stats pipe indexed knowledge directly through the aura link."

"Precisely," Meridith says. "When our ancestors realized the device could not make them mages, they turned it into a machine that casts magic on their behalf."

"What about premonition?" Kate asks. "No human spell foresees the future."

"That came from a Magium node," Meridith says. "Ancient lessathi were far more proficient at extracting secrets from the nodes than we are today. Time weavers sent by the Magium perceive glimpses of what is to come; the stat booster mimics that phenomenon on a smaller scale."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Implemented, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Implemented)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Implemented
TEXT:

"What about the two magic stats, then?" I ask. "Why are they so different from the rest?"

"They draw on the same energy reserve, but they bypass the allocation system," Meridith says. "When the prototypes had only two stats, there was no point investment interface. The device fed stored energy directly into them as it accumulated. The point system came later for the twelve new stats, and by then, development on the magic stats had been abandoned. You cannot assign points to them manually. Once I unlock the cipher, they will draw from the device's reserve and level on their own."

"Wait," I say. "Will all incoming energy go straight to magic? What happens to my point pool? Do I lose what I've banked?"

"Your banked points are safe," Meridith says. "Once energy converts into assignable points, the magic stats cannot touch it. You will still receive points for your regular stats, though the allocation rate will slow."

"It can't be worse than Ollendor," I say. "I didn't get a single point inside the city."

"Our stationary extractors ringed the major cities, where we expected contestants to congregate," Meridith says. "Once the kings refused to prohibit contestants from murdering one another in the wilds, we deployed mobile extraction squads to sweep the forests. Diane was escorting one of those units when you first ran into her."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Inefficient, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Inefficient)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Inefficient
TEXT:

"So as long as I stay clear of cities and your extraction teams, points will trickle in," I say, pulling the stat booster from my belt and handing it over. "Here. Do you need anything else to unlock them, or—"

"The device is enough," Meridith says. Her fingers dance across the keypad without looking, tapping out a rapid, practiced sequence before handing it back. "Done. The magical stats are active."

"That's all?" I stare at the screen. "I don't feel any different."

"The device is currently converting its reserve into stat levels," Meridith says. "When the first level completes, it will chime to alert you."

"Won't casting spells drain the device's energy?" Kate asks. "How will there be anything left to raise his stats?"

"The harvested energy only pays for the stat upgrades," Meridith says. "The stats themselves grant Barry the ability to channel energy from the magical plane directly, exactly like a born caster. That is why pure lessathi could never use them. Our auras contain no magic, making direct casting impossible no matter how advanced our machines become."

"If I can tap the magical plane," I say, "can I feed energy into the booster myself instead of waiting on passive extraction? There are tools for channeling mana into enchanted items. I've seen them."

"Pouring raw energy into the booster is crude and wasteful," Meridith says. "Without proper aura extraction, the charge bleeds off in minutes. You should already know that from Emergency Mode. In fact... I never asked how you triggered that mode in the first place. Spontaneous activation is extraordinarily rare. Did you find a manual bypass?"

choice(""I got the option on the screen while I was fighting some skeleton stillwaters."", B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_b3_ch11_transferred = 0)
choice(""The first time I used it, Eiden transferred his energy into the device to activate it."", B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_b3_ch11_transferred = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_transferred == 0) {
"I got the option on the screen while I was fighting some skeleton stillwaters."

"Ah," Meridith nods. "Their reserves were overflowing beyond what their auras could contain. The leakage triggered the emergency prompt."

"Exactly," I say.

"Is there any way to trigger Emergency Mode on command?" Kate asks. "If several of us poured our energy into the booster at once, would that trip the threshold?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_transferred == 1) {
"The first time I used it, Eiden transferred his energy into the device to activate it."

"Eiden?" Meridith's brow lifts. "Why would he do that? Who were you fighting?"

"Tyrath," I say. "The dragon king had us cornered. Eiden froze time and pumped power into my device to balance the fight."

"Of course," Meridith says. "He had everything to gain from seeing an ancient rival crippled. If what you say is true, Eiden's aura imprint may still reside in the hardware. Let me see it again."

I pass the booster back. Meridith touches the tip of her wand to the casing. A pulse of blue light flares across the surface, holding steady for three heartbeats before sinking back into the metal.

"Just as I suspected," Meridith says. "The device registered Eiden's aura as a compatible emergency reservoir. Whenever he is nearby, pressing this button three times in rapid succession will forcibly draw on his reserve."

"Can I drain him dry?" I ask, taking the booster back.

"Hardly," Meridith says. "Once he feels the siphon, Eiden will sever the link in seconds, and you will never catch him off guard again. But it will buy a momentary surge—enough for one or two critical spells. In a duel against him, a split second is the difference between survival and death."

"Good to know," I say. "A last resort."

"Is there any way to trigger Emergency Mode on command?" Kate asks. "If several of us poured our energy into the booster at once, would that trip the threshold?"

}
"The energy required is astronomical," Meridith says. "Only a stillwater possesses that volume of raw power, and Melindra lacks the fine control to channel it safely. The rest of you would exhaust yourselves for nothing."

"Understood," Kate says.

"Diane, you have been quiet since returning from the surface," Meridith says, turning to her second. "Is something troubling you?"

"No, Lady Meridith," Diane replies quickly. "I simply knew how critical this discussion was. I did not want to interrupt."

"Speak freely," Meridith tells her. "I have always valued your counsel."

"Thank you, Lady Meridith," Diane says. "I will speak up if there is anything to add."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Insane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Insane)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Insane
TEXT:

"Very well," Meridith says. "Our business here is concluded for now. We should find Daren and the others before they run into trouble."

We rise and follow her out into the crystal-lined corridor. Passing the concealed door to the Magium node, a thought strikes me.

"You mentioned giving us access to the node," I say, matching her stride. "How does that work in practice? How do you interface with it?"

"Our scholars developed specialized instruments over centuries to extract data safely," Meridith explains. "It is painstaking work, but rewarding. The wand I carry was engineered entirely from schematics pulled from that node, as were many of our relics."

"Are tools the only way?" Kate asks. "No direct interface?"

"Direct connection is possible, but perilous," Meridith says. "You can tether your aura straight to the node to enter the Magium realm. The access is vastly broader, but a single misstep traps your consciousness on that plane forever. To an outside observer, you remain standing with glowing yellow eyes until your body starves."

Direct aura tethering. Exactly what Arraka did during the God of Time's trial.

"What happens if someone touches the physical node?" Kate asks.

"Disaster," Meridith says, her voice darkening. "Every few years, an unauthorized lessathi slips past security to lay hands on it. Almost every one of them loses their sanity instantly. They stop speaking, refuse food and water, and turn violent if pulled away. They sit staring at the sphere until they die of dehydration."

"You left us alone in that chamber knowing that?" Kate's voice turns icy. "Without a single warning?"

"I saw no reason to give one," Meridith says defensively. "Pure lessathi feel an innate pull toward the node, requiring strict isolation. But other races show no such compulsion—not even half-lessathi. Why would anyone willingly touch a lethal anomaly without an instinct driving them?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Faction, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Faction)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Faction
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
"Ask Barry," Kate says dryly. "He touched it before we entered your meeting room."

"He touched the node?" Meridith stops dead, staring at me in disbelief. "How are you still coherent? Why haven't you lost your mind?"

"I only made contact for a second," I say. "The flood of information felt like a tidal wave hitting my skull. I yanked my hand back before it cracked."

"You resisted the pull of infinite knowledge in under a second?" Meridith shakes her head, bewildered. "If you possess that caliber of mental discipline, why do something so monumentally reckless in the first place? The more I learn about you, the less sense you make."

"Don't waste time analyzing it," Kate says. "Barry constantly hurls himself into lethal situations where a split second determines survival. Statistically, luck cannot explain his continued existence. He simply possesses an uncanny knack for making the correct gamble under extreme pressure—except when he doesn't, and blind fortune bails him out."

"I see," Meridith says slowly.

"Do we know which way the others went?" Diane asks, glancing down the hall. "The seredium dampens aura tracking completely."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
"Ask Barry," Kate says dryly. "He nearly touched it before we walked into your office."

"You did?" Meridith turns on me, eyes wide. "Why would you even consider it? Any creature without our racial compulsion should recoil on instinct!"

"Barry's survival instincts are defective," Kate says. "Do you think a sane man enters a death tournament against elite mages without knowing how to cast a spell?"

"I suppose not," Meridith murmurs.

"I pulled back in the end," I point out. "Even I have limits."

"Do we know which way the others went?" Diane asks, scanning the corridor. "The seredium dampens aura tracking completely."

}
"We passed our original entrance tunnel," Kate says. "Hadrik couldn't open that sealed door without leveling the wall, so he kept walking."

"There are only two exits from this junction," Meridith says. "One leads deeper into our territory; the other cuts straight into Heksol faction ground. We should check the Heksol corridor first."

"Isn't that tunnel marked in the common tongue?" Diane asks. "Surely he read the signs and took the other path."

choice(""Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."", B3-Ch11a-Avoided, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Avoided, v_b3_ch11_path = 0)
choice(""Actually, he might have had even more of a reason to go there, if it's marked."", B3-Ch11a-Reason, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Reason, v_b3_ch11_path = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Avoided
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch11_hear,1) if (v_hearing >= 2)
set(v_b3_ch11_hear,0) if (v_hearing < 2)
#if(v_hearing >= 2) {
"Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."

"We will check the main corridor first, then," Meridith says. "I only hope he hasn't provoked anyone."

"It's Hadrik," I say. "If he ran into someone from Heksol's crew, he probably poured them a drink."

"I hope you are right," Meridith says.

As we near the fork, Hadrik's booming laugh drifts down the passage.

"Hahaha! You know, for a Heksol guard, you're all right. Here, try this one!"

"I still have my eye on you, dwarf," a second voice answers, thick with suspicion. "Don't think a free glass makes us—wait. This is different. What's in this bottle?"

"A discerning palate!" Hadrik roars. "Subtle, but distinct. Tell you what: three bottles, three vintages. Rank them by price after one sip, and you keep the lot. What do you say?"

"Bring them out," the guard says. "If there's one thing I know, it's alcohol."

"Ha! That's the spirit!"

I look at the others. No one else reacts; the seredium walls muffle the sound for ordinary ears, but my hearing stat caught every word. He's definitely in Heksol territory.

}
#if(v_hearing < 2) {
"Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."

"We will check the main corridor first, then," Meridith says. "I only hope he hasn't provoked anyone."

"It's Hadrik," I say. "If he ran into someone from Heksol's crew, he probably poured them a drink."

"I hope you are right," Meridith says.

We take the left fork, following the carved markers toward the main lessathi living quarters.

Around the second bend, we find Daren, Flower, and Melindra squared off against a lessathi checkpoint guard.

"Listen to me," Daren says, his voice rising in exasperation. "I don't have time for bureaucratic riddles. Did a dwarf come through here or not?"

"I have already stated that a dwarf may or may not have passed this post," the guard replies evenly. "If such an individual entered, his documentation was in order. Which brings us back to yours. Present your clearance papers, or I will escort the lot of you to holding cells."

"We are Lady Meridith's guests!" Daren snaps. "How do you think we arrived? Walking through the bedrock?"

"Lady Meridith is not the only official authorized to operate the transit gate," the guard says. "Unauthorized surface entries are handled by standard protocol."

"Then ask her yourself," Daren points behind him. "She's right here."

The guard stiffens and bows low. "Lady Meridith! I was unaware of your presence today. I was detaining these trespassers who claim—"

"I heard," Meridith cuts him off. "They are with me, as is the dwarf. Did he pass through here?"

"No dwarf has crossed this post today, Lady Meridith," the guard admits. "Shall I organize a search party?"

Meridith sighs. "No. He took the Heksol tunnel. Release these humans so we can retrieve him."

"Of course," the guard says, stepping aside. "Though in the future, proper advance notification would prevent misunderstandings..."

"I will ensure the paperwork is filed," Meridith says dryly. "Let us move."

As Daren and Melindra rejoin us, Flower narrows her eyes and sticks her tongue out at the guard before turning on her heel.

}
choice("Tell the others where Hadrik went.", B3-Ch11a-Went, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Went, v_b3_ch11_reverse = 1) if (v_hearing >= 2)
choice("Remain silent, and keep going towards the wrong tunnel.", B3-Ch11a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Silent, v_b3_ch11_reverse = 0) if (v_hearing >= 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Observation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Observation) if (v_perception >= 4 && v_hearing < 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Behind, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Behind) if (v_hearing < 2 && v_perception < 4)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Silent
TEXT:

I keep quiet. Hadrik sounded like he had the situation well under control, and interrupting a dwarf midway through an ale contest never ends well.

We follow the left fork toward the primary lessathi sector.

Around the second bend, we find Daren, Flower, and Melindra squared off against a lessathi checkpoint guard.

"Listen to me," Daren says, his voice rising in exasperation. "I don't have time for bureaucratic riddles. Did a dwarf come through here or not?"

"I have already stated that a dwarf may or may not have passed this post," the guard replies evenly. "If such an individual entered, his documentation was in order. Which brings us back to yours. Present your clearance papers, or I will escort the lot of you to holding cells."

"We are Lady Meridith's guests!" Daren snaps. "How do you think we arrived? Walking through the bedrock?"

"Lady Meridith is not the only official authorized to operate the transit gate," the guard says. "Unauthorized surface entries are handled by standard protocol."

"Then ask her yourself," Daren points behind him. "She's right here."

The guard stiffens and bows low. "Lady Meridith! I was unaware of your presence today. I was detaining these trespassers who claim—"

"I heard," Meridith cuts him off. "They are with me, as is the dwarf. Did he pass through here?"

"No dwarf has crossed this post today, Lady Meridith," the guard admits. "Shall I organize a search party?"

Meridith sighs. "No. He took the Heksol tunnel. Release these humans so we can retrieve him."

"Of course," the guard says, stepping aside. "Though in the future, proper advance notification would prevent misunderstandings..."

"I will ensure the paperwork is filed," Meridith says dryly. "Let us move."

As Daren and Melindra rejoin us, Flower narrows her eyes and sticks her tongue out at the guard before turning on her heel.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Observation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Observation) if (v_perception >= 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Behind, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Behind) if (v_perception < 4)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Observation
TEXT:

As we turn to leave, a section of the seredium masonry catches my eye. The seam doesn't match the surrounding bedrock.

"Barry?" Daren pauses. "What is it?"

"This stonework," I say, running my fingers over the edge. The texture is smoother, cut with unnatural precision. "This tunnel was engineered by lessathi hands, wasn't it?"

"These shafts predate living memory," Meridith says. "Why does that matter?"

"An ancient lessathi architect left puzzle pieces hidden across every site he constructed," I say. "Given that this is the primary lessathi redoubt, this wall looks suspicious. Melindra, Flower—take a look."

"We do not have time for games," Meridith protests. "Your companion could be in the middle of a brawl with Heksol guards—"

"Hadrik is fine," I assure her.

Melindra steps up and taps the stone with a knuckle. "The masonry is artificial, but I don't feel a mechanical latch. Flower?"

"I know a few old ruin knock codes," Flower says brightly. "Let me try!"

"This is absurd," Meridith mutters. "It will take hours."

"Watch," Flower says. She raps a rhythmic sequence against the seam, pauses, then knocks again. "Two down. Three left!"

On the third sequence, the stone slab grinds backward into the wall and slides aside. A gray rectangular block emerges in its place, engraved in the common tongue: 'If you like discovering secrets, place your palm upon this rock.'

Flower gasps, reading the script. "Barry, you were right! It's the architect!"

She presses her palm against the stone. With a soft click, a metal puzzle token ejects from a narrow slit beneath the inscription, followed by a sliver of parchment reading: 'Find all of the pieces of the puzzle, and I will share with you my greatest secret. I've left one of these in each of the buildings I've designed.'

"Barry, hurry!" Flower urges. "Put your hand on it before it resets!"

choice("Place my palm on the stone.", B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 = 1)
choice("Refuse to take a puzzle piece for myself.", B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 = 0)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Heksol
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch11_fourth,1) if (v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 1)
achievement("Fourth piece of the puzzle",v_ac_b3_ch11_fourth)
#if(v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 1) {
I place my palm flat against the carved surface. A matching puzzle piece drops into my hand with a duplicate parchment. The mechanism clicks, and the gray block withdraws, sealing the seredium wall flush once more.

"Satisfied?" Meridith asks, checking the corridor. "Can we proceed?"

"All done," I say, pocketing the piece. "Lead the way."

"Let us hope Hadrik was halted at the perimeter," Meridith says.

We backtrack to the junction and head down the right tunnel into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice carries around the bend.

"Sounds like your friend and I would have seen eye to eye," Hadrik is saying. "Pity I missed him."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 0) {
"I don't need one," I say. "I only pointed it out because you're collecting them."

"Really?" Flower's eyes widen. "Thanks, Barry! I owe you!"

"Don't worry about it," I say as the mechanism resets, sliding the stone flush with the wall.

"Satisfied?" Meridith asks, checking the corridor. "Can we proceed?"

"All done," I say. "Lead the way."

"Let us hope Hadrik was halted at the perimeter," Meridith says.

We backtrack to the junction and head down the right tunnel into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice carries around the bend.

"Sounds like your friend and I would have seen eye to eye," Hadrik is saying. "Pity I missed him."

}
"He died a proper dwarf death," the guard replies. "Picked a drunken brawl with the wrong crew and took a blade through the ribs. Dragged four of them down with him before he dropped. Always said a tavern fight was the only way to go, so he got his wish."

We round the corner. Hadrik sits on an upturned crate, surrounded by empty dwarven ale bottles, raising a fresh glass with a grinning lessathi guard.

"Well, look who decided to join the party!" Hadrik calls out, waving a bottle. "The high-and-mighty boss herself! Don't just stand there, Meridith—pull up a rock and have a drink!"

"I must decline," Meridith says stiffly. "I have critical meetings that require a clear head."

"Is she always this stiff?" Hadrik asks the guard, refilling both cups.

"Every waking minute," the guard replies, taking a deep draught.

"Hadrik, damn it," Daren groans. "Do you know what we went through looking for you? The checkpoint guard down the other corridor nearly threw us in a cell!"

"Not my fault you look like bandits," Hadrik laughs. "Look at me. I ran into a guard and we're getting along great."

"Don't give me ideas, dwarf," the guard chuckles, clinking his mug against Hadrik's.

"Spare me the posturing, Kleykarth," Meridith says cold. "I came to ensure my guest hadn't started a riot in your sector. Clearly, my concern was misplaced."

"The dwarf had far more grievance with you than with us, 'Lady' Meridith," Kleykarth sneers. "He shared some choice thoughts about your leadership over the first round. Care to hear them?"

"I would be fascinated," Meridith says, fixing Hadrik with a sharp gaze.

"No need, no need!" Hadrik claps a heavy hand on the guard's shoulder.

"Hahaha!" Kleykarth barks a laugh. "Just pulling your beard, dwarf. Your secrets are safe with me."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Uverg, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Uverg)


